-The phrase "use our design system" hides several different sources. A
-Figma library may define component anatomy and variants. A React
-package may define actual props and accessibility behavior. A shipped
-screen may reveal composition rules that appear nowhere in
-documentation. Product code and APIs determine what the interface can
-really do.
-Claude can read several of these sources. It cannot resolve an unspoken
-conflict between them. The team needs a declared hierarchy before
-generation begins.
-Build the source-of-truth stack
-Use five layers:
- 1
-Production behavior. The current application, routes, permissions,
-persistence, data contracts, and tested interaction rules.
- 2
-Production component code. The components, props, tokens,
-accessibility semantics, and imports that engineers can actually ship.
- 3
-Published design library. Component anatomy, variants, variables,
-layouts, and design guidance.
- 4
-Representative shipped screens. Evidence for composition, density,
-hierarchy, responsive behavior, and local conventions.
- 5
-Product intent. The approved problem, target user, success journey,
-non-goals, and acceptance tests for the change.
-When two layers disagree, record the conflict. Do not let Claude select a
-winner through visual improvisation.
